e":"all","minUnitsEach":5,"letter":true,"minGrade":"A-","items":[["POLISCI 330A"],["POLISCI 437C"],["POLISCI 432R"]]}]}]},{"id":"ov-third","t":"any","name":"Third field","children":[{"id":"ov-third-ir","t":"courses","name":"International Relations (two core or elective classes)","n":2,"mode":"atLeast","minUnitsEach":3,"letter":true,"items":[["POLISCI 410A"],["POLISCI 410B"],["POLISCI 410C"],["ECON 266"],["ECON 267"],["ECON 268"],["ECON 269"],["HISTORY 301A"],["HISTORY 302G"],["HISTORY 338J"],["HISTORY 356"],["LAW 5010"],["LAW 5013"],["LAW 5014"],["LAW 5019A"],["POLISCI 311N"],["POLISCI 460A"],["SOC 309"],["SOC 378"]]},{"id":"ov-third-am","t":"courses","name":"American Politics (two core or elective classes)","n":2,"mode":"atLeast","minUnitsEach":3,"letter":true,"items":[["POLISCI 420A"],["POLISCI 420B"],["ECON 229"],["POLECON 680"],["POLECON 682"],["POLISCI 351B"],["POLISCI 320R"],["POLISCI 324L"],["POLISCI 326"],["POLISCI 327C"],["POLISCI 421K"],["POLISCI 422F"],["POLISCI 421A"],["POLISCI 423A"],["POLISCI 423B"],["POLISCI 424C"],["POLISCI 426"],["POLISCI 427C"],["POLISCI 460A"],["POLISCI 461"],["SOC 340"]]},{"id":"ov-third-cp","t":"courses","name":"Comparative Politics (two core or elective classes)","n":2,"mode":"atLeast","minUnitsEach":3,"letter":true,"items":[["POLISCI 440A"],["POLISCI 440B"],["ECON 229"],["POLECON 680"],["POLECON 682"],["POLISCI 344U"],["POLISCI 347G"],["POLISCI 348"],["POLISCI 348D"],["POLISCI 348S"],["POLISCI 351B"],["POLISCI 443S"],["POLISCI 443T"],["POLISCI 444A"],["POLISCI 445C"],["POLISCI 446"],["POLISCI 446A"],["POLISCI 460A"],["POLISCI 461"]]},{"id":"ov-third-qm","t":"courses","name":"Quantitative Methodology (POLISCI 450C and 450D)","mode":"all","minUnitsEach":3,"letter":true,"items":[["POLISCI 450C"],["POLISCI 450D"]]},{"id":"ov-third-ft","t":"courses","name":"Formal Theory (POLISCI 356A and 356B)","mode":"all","minUnitsEach":3,"letter":true,"items":[["POLISCI 356A"],["POLISCI 356B"]]},{"id":"ov-third-pt","t":"courses","name":"Political Theory (two core classes)","n":2,"mode":"atLeast","minUnitsEach":3,"letter":true,"items":[["POLISCI 330A"],["POLISCI 437C"],["POLISCI 432R"]]},{"id":"ov-third-self","t":"text","name":"Self-designed third field (two courses approved by two Political Science faculty)","n":2,"manual":"approval"}]},{"id":"ov-fields-distinct","t":"text","name":"First, second and third fields are different (not both Formal Theory and Quantitative Methodology)","manual":"approval"},{"id":"ov-prospectus","t":"text","name":"Dissertation prospectus approved and presented by the end of the third year","manual":"milestone"}],"reviewed":true}]},{"id":"PGtlOm3w","name":"Teaching Requirement","rules":[{"id":"BEf85sm7","desc":"Teaching is core to the academic and professional training of doctoral students in our program. All students are required to complete a minimum of three quarters of teaching experience by the end of their fourth year in the program regardless of their financial support. Most students are typically expected to complete at least two additional quarters of teaching beyond the minimum required for the degree to further their pedagogical and professional skills development and be eligible for financial support. PhD students in the Political Science department should expect to teach a combined total of 5 quarters by the end of the fifth year of residency in the program.","t":"text","manual":"milestone","name":"Three quarters of teaching","reviewed":true}]},{"id":"MpK9cbqm","name":"Advancement to Candidacy","rules":[{"id":"l4oXA3cP","desc":"Admission to candidacy is a prospective judgement by the faculty of a student’s potential to successfully complete the requirements of the degree program. Students are expected to complete qualifying procedures and apply for candidacy by the spring quarter of their second year in the program. Qualifying procedures in the Department of Political Science include satisfactory completion of required coursework, passing a comprehensive examination in the student’s first field, and submission of an approved field paper. Additional details on qualifying procedures can be found in the PhD Program Guide.","t":"text","manual":"exam","name":"First-field comprehensive exam and field paper","reviewed":true}]},{"id":"ofqDwlr6","name":"Oral Exam","rules":[{"id":"ui9WrF2k","desc":"In the Department of Political Science, the oral exam is a defense of the dissertation presented upon the completion of a substantial portion of the dissertation or upon completion of a pre-final draft. Students consult with their dissertation advisor and reading committee members on the timing of the defense. Students are expected to complete this milestone by the end of their fifth year in the program.","t":"text","manual":"exam","name":"Oral exam (dissertation defense)","reviewed":true}]},{"id":"iwHWtFFx","name":"Dissertation","rules":[{"id":"uA5ZEb35","desc":"Students must form a dissertation reading committee by the beginning of their fourth year. Students are required to submit an approved dissertation that makes an original contribution to scholarship or scientific knowledge in the field of Political Science. Students are expected to submit the dissertation in the same quarter that they complete the oral exam, and no later than the subsequent quarter.","t":"text","manual":"thesis","name":"Dissertation","reviewed":true}]}],"unitsMin":135,"grading":"Students are required to earn grades of B or better in all courses take each term. While a B is the minimum required grade for all classes, all students must earn a minimum grade of A- for courses taken to fulfill first and second field requirements. Grades of B- and below are inadequate and are reviewed by the faculty. The student may be required to revise and resubmit work associated with the course or retake the course.","transferPolicy":"Students who completed graduate coursework at another institution are encouraged to transfer course units in winter quarter of their first year of doctoral study. The Director of Graduate Studies will evaluate the transfer of units on a course-by-course basis. Graduate work accepted for transfer of residency does not automatically exempt a student from having to complete a course requirement for the degree.","reviewed":"2026-10-23","reviewNotes":"You complete the program courses (POLISCI 450A and 450B, a political theory course, a research design course, each for 5 units, and POLISCI 480 with an S) and the courses for a first, second and third field, all letter graded. With Comparative Politics as your first field, your research design course must be POLISCI 440C. Every course needs a B or better, and first- and second-field courses an A- or better. That your fields differ, the self-designed third field, the prospectus, teaching, the first-field exam and field paper, the oral exam and the dissertation are checked by hand.","minGrade":"B"}